VERDICT: STRONGLY-FAVOURS
  scope: Traced the FCDP drafting protocol end-to-end in archon-cli — from `archon evidence curate` / the `archon draft` wizard through pack assembly,
  scope: the draft-time bank gate, the D1→D1.5→D2→gauntlet→R-loop orchestrator, «Qnn» substitution, and the post-run provenance import — naming every
  scope: function and recording what provenance is carried and dropped at each hop. Read crates/archon-draft in full
  scope: (src/{lib,fable,gauntlet,judge,orchestrator,provenance}.rs, src/bin/archon_fcdp.rs, tests/{golden,orchestrator}.rs, tests/fixtures/m6/*),
  scope: crates/archon-evidence/src/{lib,pack,verify,quote_trim,index}.rs, src/command/{curate,curate_agent,evidence,draft}.rs, crates/archon-
  scope: docs/src/quote_verify.rs, plus plans/fable-console-drafting-protocol-v2.md and both plans/fcdp-archon-port-plan*.md. Ran read-only `archon
  scope: docs verify-quote` and `archon evidence find` (with and without --index) against the live 942 MB store. This agent establishes the VALUE side
  scope: only; I did not re-audit the incumbent compiler/runtime beyond confirming whether an FCDP implementation exists there.

  PORT COST:
      Two separable costs; I size the first two and refuse to invent the third.  (1) THREADING PROVENANCE THROUGH THE EXISTING PACK PATH — 300-500
      lines across 5 structs in 5 files, plus golden-fixture regeneration. Add {char_start, char_end} to QuoteFragment (crates/archon-
      docs/src/quote_verify.rs:33-42, values already computed at :167-170), stop discarding FragmentLocator and similarity at
      src/command/curate.rs:44-107, carry chunk_id/bbox/document_id through CuratedQuote (crates/archon-evidence/src/pack.rs:20-41, which already
      carries document_id), widen QuoteEntry (crates/archon-draft/src/lib.rs:169-173) additively with #[serde(default)]. BASIS: commit 4c581761
      "feat(evidence): Pack assembly" = 250 insertions / 4 files; commit 3e689c7f "bank re-verification" = 298 insertions / 4 files; commit ac7bd3b4
      "trim pack quotes to claim-relevant sentence spans" = 387 insertions / 5 files. This work is the same shape and scale as all three. Add fixture
      cost: provenance.rs declare() output is byte-asserted (tests/golden.rs:169-188) and five gauntlet reference JSONs are equality-checked
      (golden.rs:55-125).  (2) CLAIM-LEVEL STORE + READER + QUERY SURFACE IN ARCHON — 900-1,400 lines. BASIS: commit 6cd6691a "feat(evidence):
      compiled research index — canonical expansion, owning-work routing, tension surfacing" = 857 insertions / 6 files delivered the directly
      analogous reader-plus-routing layer (now 977 lines at crates/archon-evidence/src/index.rs), and crates/archon-docs/src/schema.rs is the in-repo
      precedent for a CozoDB relation module. Add ~150-250 lines to thread claim/tension material into build_head and d15_prompt (orchestrator.rs:67,
      :127), on the additive-Pack-field precedent set by DraftType (lib.rs:45-64, :48 serde default, round-trip test at :652).  (3) CLAIM EXTRACTION
      AT 45,000-CLAIM / 450,000-CLAUSE SCALE — NO ESTIMATE. Nothing in archon-cli extracts claims or clauses today; ingestion produces chunks, blocks,
      page breaks and locators only. No commit in this repo is comparable, so any number I gave would be invented. What I can say with evidence: this
      cost is repo-independent — it is new work whether it lands in Rust or in Python — so it does not discriminate between the two hosts. What DOES
      discriminate is that building it in claudeflow-testing additionally requires re-implementing (1) and (2), i.e. a second copy of the index
      consumer (977 lines already written in Rust) and a second copy of the span prover (crates/archon-docs/src/quote_verify.rs, backed by a 942 MB
      store with 224 documents), to feed a drafting protocol that has no implementation in that repo at all.

  SUMMARY:
      The user's statement that "the FCDP drafting protocol pulls its quotations from archon" is literally true and stronger than stated: FCDP has NO
      implementation in claudeflow-testing at all. The only FCDP artifact there is the 43-line scripts/substitute-quote-ids.py plus the plan
      documents; `find . -name "*.py" | grep -iE "fcdp|gauntlet|judge"` returns nothing else. The entire gate-enforced pipeline — pack schema, G-P,
      «Qnn» substitution, the seven-gate gauntlet, the G-E/G-G judges, the R-loop, the hash chain, the disclosure declaration — is 3,686 lines of Rust
      in crates/archon-draft, driven from src/command/draft.rs. Quotations reach a draft through exactly one path: assemble_pack (pack.rs:72) →
      trim_to_relevant_span (pack.rs:92) → reanchor_cites (pack.rs:165) → write_pack (pack.rs:236) → preflight_draft_inputs/verify_bank (draft.rs:972,
      verify.rs:130) → orchestrator::run (orchestrator.rs:412) → substitute_quote_ids (draft lib.rs:246), which is the single hop where quoted words
      enter the prose.  All three assertions are CONFIRMED. QuoteEntry is exactly `{text, cite}` (draft lib.rs:169-173) — no chunk_id, no offsets, no
      bbox, no match_kind. Verification is `{quotes_verified_at: String, verified_against: Vec<String>, test_fixture: bool}` (lib.rs:158-166); in the
      real m6 pack `verified_against` holds two free-text notes naming PDF paths and extraction method — nothing machine-checks them. gp_validate
      (lib.rs:178-234) takes no db handle and never touches the corpus: it checks P4a↔P4b id bijection, non-empty text/evidence/locks/usage, and
      `quotes_verified_at == today`. One important CORRECTION to the framing: gp_validate is not the only gate on the drafting path. `archon draft`
      runs preflight_draft_inputs (draft.rs:972), which calls archon_evidence::verify_bank and BLOCKS the run on any non-Exact status including
      NearVerbatim (verify.rs:88-101, H-9). So a content re-check against the live corpus DOES exist today — it is just not in gp_validate, it is
      skipped for test_fixture packs, it degrades to a warning when the corpus is absent (draft.rs ~1046), and the standalone archon-fcdp binary
      bypasses it entirely (bin/archon_fcdp.rs:116-173).  The provenance loss is severe and is a pure field-width problem, not a missing-capability
      problem. quote_verify computes exact char offsets (a,b) at quote_verify.rs:167-170, threads them into build_location's local_of closure to
      narrow page and bbox, then discards them because QuoteFragment (quote_verify.rs:33-42) has no offset field. curate.rs:44 then collapses the
      whole QuoteLocation into a ReviewItem, dropping the FragmentLocator (the Bekker number — the citation-grade locus) entirely and reducing
      similarity to the string "exact-1.00" or "fuzzy". curate.rs:174-215 then drops bbox, chunk_id and page_boxes, hardcodes `intended_use:
      "evidential grounding"`, and writes the RA's one-line claim into BOTH `description` and `evidence` — so G-E item E4 ("Is each quotation used in
      a way consistent with its stated intended rhetorical use?", judge.rs:54) judges every RA-curated pack against a constant. The RA's per-quote
      `rationale` reaches stdout (evidence.rs:343) and the --json dump (evidence.rs:401) and nothing else. By the time the pack is on disk, a quote is
      two strings.  The value of a claim-level index living in archon is demonstrable rather than theoretical, and the live run shows it exactly.
      `archon evidence find "rhetorical incorporation"` returns 10/10 candidates from Bitzer's "The Rhetorical Situation", every one OCR-corrupted
      ("pnractical", "consiaered", "moditication"), none about incorporation. With `--index` the compiled index matches the concept "Incorporation"
      and work-boosts Calleja's In-Game into the top two slots — but the chunks it surfaces are "mimetic control" (p.64, provenance unresolved) and
      "macro Involvernent" (p.38). The work-level index gets the right BOOK and cannot get the right SENTENCE. Meanwhile the m6 pack's Q1 — the
      definitional passage — verifies live at exact-1.00, p.169, bbox [58,365,375,487]. A human found that sentence; the pipeline cannot. That gap is
      precisely what claim-level granularity closes, and archon already owns both halves it would connect: the index consumer (archon-
      evidence/src/index.rs, 977 lines, already reading compiled-index.json) and the span prover (quote_verify, already returning sub-span-resolved
      page and bbox).  Concretely, a claim-level index in archon lets a pack carry what it cannot today: QuoteEntry widened with {document_id,
      chunk_id, char_start, char_end, page, bbox, match_kind, similarity} — every field already computed and thrown away; a typed FragmentLocator
      instead of a formatted locus string; real per-claim intended_use and distinct description/evidence instead of one duplicated note; index
      tension-edge material bound to the specific claim for D1.5's counterargument pass (today TensionEdge and Hook are parsed at index.rs:71-100 and
      used only for query expansion — build_head and d15_prompt carry none of it); and content hashes from doc_chunk_hashes instead of free-text
      verification notes. It also enables four gates that cannot run today: (1) offset-anchored re-verification, which catches the case that currently
      passes silently — the quote is verbatim somewhere in the cited document but not at the passage it was curated from, because find_fragment_bboxes
      takes the FIRST exact substring match (quote_verify.rs:167) and cannot tell "my sentence" from "the same words 300 pages later"; (2) a
      mechanical claim-fidelity check, replacing part of the LLM-judged E2/E3/E5 battery whose truncation failure mode is documented as L7 in the port
      plan; (3) a cleaning-version/re-ingest staleness gate keyed on raw_sha256 rather than a calendar date; (4) a coverage gate over contested
      claims, so "every weakness" is checkable rather than detected by substring-searching the model's own skeleton for "SURFACE-TO-USER"
      (orchestrator.rs:484).  Building the overhaul in claudeflow-testing means building a second copy of the index consumer and a second copy of the
      span prover, in a repo whose .archon store is 28 KB and empty, to feed a drafting protocol that does not exist there. The honest counterweight
      is corpus quality, not architecture: the Bitzer hits show heavy OCR corruption and `has_bbox` was false on every one of them, so 450,000 clause
      records extracted over that text will inherit the corruption, and claim records must tolerate bbox:None.

