VERDICT: STRONGLY-FAVOURS
  scope: Read crates/archon-evidence/src/index.rs in full (977 lines, incl. all 12 unit tests + 2 inline fixtures), plus quote_verify.rs (596),
  scope: archon-docs/src/schema.rs, archon-knowledge (schema.rs, claim_extractor.rs, store.rs), archon-draft/src/lib.rs
  scope: (Pack/QuoteEntry/Verification/gp_validate), archon-evidence/src/pack.rs (CuratedQuote), the verify-quote JSON emitter (src/command/docs.rs)
  scope: and provenance_json (src/command/evidence.rs). On the incumbent side: read the live 664 KB compiled-index.json field-by-field,
  scope: scripts/compile-corpus-index.py structure + TEXT_DIRS, and the three live TS consumers (jsonl-loaders.ts loader, corpus-index-provider.ts,
  scope: cross-author-utils.ts). Ran archon live read-only: docs status, evidence find --index (hybrid + exact). No writes, no builds.

  PORT COST:
      ~1,200–1,400 new Rust LOC for the recommended path; ~2,600–3,000 if all markdown scrapers must be ported too. Breakdown with basis: (1)
      char_start/char_end exposure — 4 files, ~10 lines, values already in scope (measured, see finding). (2) Legacy-view emitter — add `Serialize` +
      7 missing fields to the 4 raw structs at index.rs:36-107 (72 lines today) + an emit fn + one CLI arm; basis = the existing verify-quote JSON
      emitter at src/command/docs.rs:139-165 is 27 lines for a comparable nested shape, and crates/archon-evidence/src/pack.rs already has write_pack
      as the artifact-writing precedent → ~250 LOC + ~100 LOC golden-file tests. (3) Claim + clause Cozo relations — 2 `ensure_*` fns modeled exactly
      on schema.rs:193-210 (ensure_doc_chunk_blocks, 18 lines) and schema.rs:215-224 (10 lines) → ~40 LOC schema + ~200 LOC store put/list modeled on
      store::list_chunk_blocks_for_chunk. (4) Structured-JSON reader for the 12 *-ontology.json files (2 shapes: book-level with
      concepts/dimensions/positions/notable_tensions, cluster with nodes) → serde structs + mapper, ~400-600 LOC. (5) Claim-grounding driver (claim
      text → find_fragment_bboxes → persist span) → ~200-300 LOC; basis = crates/archon-evidence/src/lib.rs:362 already performs exactly this locate-
      and-join for candidates. EXCLUDED and separately estimated: porting the 5 markdown parsers, compile-corpus-index.py lines 195-1176 = ~980 Python
      lines across 7 layouts → ~1,300-1,600 Rust LOC. That last figure is my weakest estimate: there is no comparable Python→Rust port already in the
      repo to calibrate the ratio against, so I am applying a generic 1.3-1.6x and flagging it as such. Note the 15 entry dirs with no structured JSON
      would need this path (or new authored JSON) to reach parity.

  SUMMARY:
      Archon-as-producer is a real migration path, not wishful thinking — but for a reason sharper than "Rust is better". The compiled artifact's
      three fatal absences (no id, no page, no quote/locus on any node) are exactly the three things archon already computes and already discards.
      quote_verify.rs computes char offsets (a,b) at line 163-166, narrows page through doc_chunk_page_breaks and bbox through doc_chunk_blocks,
      resolves a Bekker/PageNumber locator off the running head — then throws the offsets away because QuoteFragment has no field for them. Exposing
      them is a 2-field change across 4 files, ~10 added lines, with the values already in scope at the construction site. Meanwhile the incumbent
      compiler ignores 12 structured *-ontology.json files that ALREADY carry canonical_id, page_anchors{unit, book_page}, tier, type, and an explicit
      `positions` array of claim records — i.e. the id/page/claim layer the artifact lacks is sitting on disk unread. A Rust producer that reads those
      12 JSON files and grounds each claim through the existing find_fragment_bboxes gets id + exact char span + page + bbox + locator + match_kind
      per claim, which no amount of work on the Python scraper can produce because the Python discards offsets in the first statement of every parser
      and has no corpus text at all. The legacy view is cheap and safe: RawIndex/RawNode/Hook/TensionEdge already model the artifact's 4 arrays; they
      are missing only 7 optional String fields and the Serialize derive. Two caveats that are real, not cosmetic: (1) the two repos ALREADY hold
      divergent copies of compiled-index.json today (670/1663 in archon vs 701/1722 in claudeflow-testing, different md5) — archon-as-producer fixes
      this rather than causing it; (2) the full 45k-claim/450k-clause corpus can never round-trip through the legacy JSON view (~121 MB projected),
      but it does not need to: the TS renders at most 12 nodes + 3 hooks + 5 tensions per prompt, so the legacy view is correctly a bounded
      projection.

