VERDICT: STRONGLY-FAVOURS
  scope: Storage-layer feasibility for the corpus-index overhaul: can the archon-cli CozoDB store hold ~45,000 claim + ~450,000 clause records with
  scope: exact provenance? Read crates/archon-docs/src/schema.rs in full and crates/archon-cozo/ to establish every declared relation, the storage
  scope: engine, indexing, FTS and vector support; measure the live 987 MB store read-only; confirm doc_chunk_blocks actually has rows by tracing
  scope: quote_verify.rs fragment_for(); project to 450,000 clause records and compare against the incumbent's measured ceilings. Read-only throughout
  scope: — no builds, no writes, no git write operations.

  PORT COST:
      Roughly 1,000-1,600 lines of new Rust plus ~75 lines of query rewrites, against the review's ~1,700 lines slated for replacement in the
      incumbent (~1,100 of scripts/compile-corpus-index.py plus ~600 of the TS matching layer). Breakdown with basis, all from comparable prior work
      in this repo: (a) 5 new Cozo relations, ~80 lines — basis: schema.rs:193-210 defines doc_chunk_blocks, the most complex existing relation, in 18
      lines, and the whole 18-relation schema.rs is 644 lines including 8 tests; (b) batched store layer for those relations, ~250 lines — basis:
      archon-docs/src/store.rs:508-545 is 38 lines per batched writer, and archon-knowledge/src/store.rs is 402 lines for 5 relations in the unbatched
      style; (c) prefix-pushdown query rewrites, ~75 lines — basis: 24 `= $param` sites in archon-docs/src/store.rs at ~3 lines each; (d) offsets
      plumbed through QuoteFragment and archon-draft's QuoteEntry, ~150 lines — basis: QuoteFragment is 11 lines, fragment_for is 46
      (quote_verify.rs:367-412), build_location already threads matched_span (:213-290); (e) offset-preserving claim/clause extractor, 400-600 lines —
      basis: the existing heuristic claim_extractor.rs is 171 lines and lacks offsets, clause decomposition and any real confidence model, so ~3x; (f)
      guard + retry wiring for archon-knowledge, ~40 lines — basis: archon-docs/src/cozo_retry.rs is 60 lines including tests, of which the delegating
      wrapper is 8. NOT included and identical in either repo: the semantic quality of extraction, and the reingest needed to lift spatial coverage
      above 63.5%. Storage cost of the result: ~200-260 MB added to a 987 MB store (basis: doc_locators measures 232 B/row for a text-bearing record;
      a kb_claims-shaped row with provenance columns is ~450-550 B; 495,000 rows x ~500 B), taking row count from 2,751,189 to ~3.25M — a +18%
      increase on a store whose largest relation full-scans in 80 ms.

  SUMMARY:
      The CozoDB store can hold the target volume with room to spare, and — more decisively — it already holds the one thing the overhaul is actually
      about: exact character-level provenance. Verified: the store is a single SQLite table `cozo(k BLOB primary key, v BLOB)` (cozo-ce
      0.7.13-alpha.3, features storage-sqlite), 2,751,189 rows / 987,086,848 bytes / 240,988 pages, page_size 4096, journal_mode DELETE, freelist 0. I
      decoded the Cozo catalogue (relation id 0, 27 rows) and mapped every relation id to its row count. doc_chunk_blocks IS populated: 114,612 rows /
      26,360,674 bytes (230 B/row); doc_chunk_page_breaks 27,227 rows; doc_chunk_hashes 17,819; doc_chunk_spatial 11,311; doc_locators 2,961;
      doc_chunks 17,824 / 56,023,036 bytes of content; the FTS inverted index 2,475,077 rows / 465,776,028 bytes. The sub-span bbox is real and block-
      driven: `archon docs verify-quote "the soul is in a way all existing things"` returns EXACT, p.48, bbox [66,327,360,368] (41pt tall) with
      locator 431b1 (Bekker), while the two fuzzy hits fall back to full-chunk boxes 519pt and 472pt tall — exactly the fragment_for() branch at
      quote_verify.rs:373-394, which calls store::list_chunk_blocks_for_chunk (quote_verify.rs:377) and narrows via sub_span_bbox
      (quote_verify.rs:343-361) only when blocks are non-empty.  Adding 45,000 claims + 450,000 clauses is a +18% row-count increase (2.75M -> ~3.25M)
      and, at doc_locators' measured 232 B/row for a text-bearing record scaled to ~450-550 B for a claim/clause row, roughly +200-260 MB on a 987 MB
      store. That is not close to any engine limit. SQLite's own ceiling is 281 TB; the measured full-scan rate on this machine is ~31M key-rows/s
      (2,475,077 rows in 80 ms; 114,612 in 3.84 ms), a point lookup is 0.07 ms and a prefix range scan of 2,399 rows is 0.19 ms. Crucially, my probe
      used the exact SQL Cozo's sqlite backend issues — cozo-ce-0.7.13-alpha.3/src/storage/sqlite.rs:131-139 defines QUERIES as `select v from cozo
      where k = ?`, `select k, v from cozo where k >= ? and k < ? order by k`, `select count(*) from cozo where k >= ? and k < ?` — so these are the
      storage primitives, not analogues.  Against that, the incumbent format cannot hold the target at all. corpus/index/compiled-index.json is
      666,303 bytes for 2,559 records: ontologyNodes 701 at 649 B/record, crossPipelineHooks 72 at 533 B, tensionEdges 64 at 682 B, canonicalTerms
      1,722 at 27 B — and those 649-byte node records carry no id, page, quote or locus. 450,000 clause records with provenance at >=650 B/record is
      ~292 MB in one file, loaded by src/god-agent/shared/jsonl-loaders.ts:210-211 as `readFileSync(indexPath, 'utf-8')` then `JSON.parse(raw)` into a
      module-level cache — a whole-file JS string plus a parsed object graph typically 5-10x the text, in every tsx process. That is ~18x past the
      review's measured 24.97x loader ceiling (~16.6 MB), before the 157 tsc errors and zero tests are considered.  The archon side is not free of
      defects, but every one is implementation-level and fixable inside the repo, whereas the incumbent's are format-level. The important one: Cozo's
      StoredRA::iter (query/ra.rs:1502-1509) is `self.storage.scan_all(tx)` plus a post-filter, with no compute_bounds — compute_bounds appears only
      inside prefix_join (ra.rs:1348). And compile.rs:601 turns `chunk_id = $cid` into `ret.filter(build_equate(...))` when chunk_id is already bound
      by the preceding atom. So archon's standard query form `*doc_chunk_blocks{...}, chunk_id = $cid` (store.rs:554-556) is a FULL RELATION SCAN
      today, and there are 24 such `= $param` filter lines in archon-docs/src/store.rs alone. At 114,612 rows that costs single-digit ms and nobody
      noticed; at 450,000 clause rows / ~200 MB it becomes ~50 ms warm per lookup and far worse cold. The engine has the fast path —
      scan_bounded_prefix, measured at 0.19 ms — archon's query style just does not reach it. Second: archon-knowledge/src/store.rs:77-87 issues one
      db.run_script per claim, and with journal_mode=DELETE and no synchronous pragma that is one fsync-bounded transaction per row; 45,000 claims
      would be 45,000 transactions. archon-docs already has the correct batched pattern (store.rs:508-545, one multi-row `:put` over `$rows`). Third:
      archon-knowledge has no archon-cozo dependency and makes 7 raw db.run_script calls, so its writes bypass the retry/lock guard that every archon-
      docs write goes through (cozo_retry.rs:20-28).  The decisive asymmetry is not capacity, it is that archon already computes the offsets the goal
      requires and merely fails to persist them, while the incumbent never has them at all. quote_verify.rs:162-166 computes (a,b) via find_subslice,
      local_span_in_chunk (quote_verify.rs:297-311) computes (lo,hi), both are used to narrow page and bbox, and then QuoteFragment
      (quote_verify.rs:32-42) has no field to carry them. Adding two usize fields there is a smaller change than any part of the incumbent's
      replacement, and it lands on a store that already has 114,612 block rows to anchor against.

