VERDICT: FAVOURS
  scope: Engineering-health comparison of /home/dalton/projects/archon-cli (Rust, 39 crates) vs /home/dalton/projects/claudeflow-testing (Python
  scope: compiler + TypeScript runtime) as the home for the corpus-index overhaul (~45,000 claims / ~450,000 clause records with per-clause
  scope: provenance). Read-only: greps, file reads, git log/ls-tree/ls-files, `npx tsc --noEmit`, and read-only `archon docs status` / `archon docs
  scope: verify-quote` against the prebuilt debug binary. No cargo invoked, nothing written to either repo.

  PORT COST:
      Roughly 4,000-6,500 new Rust lines across two crates (archon-docs schema additions + a new or extended archon-evidence/archon-corpus), of which
      2,500-4,000 is adapter work that costs the same in any language. Bases, each measured: schema.rs is 644 lines for 20 CozoDB relations (~32
      lines/relation) and the migration spec names ~14 new ones => ~450 lines; the TS query surface being replaced is 816 lines (corpus-index-
      provider.ts 202 + cross-author-utils.ts 614) => ~600-900 Rust; the incumbent's five parsers are ~880 lines and cover 2.7% of the 1,856 files in
      corpus/index, so full coverage of 883 .md + 271 .csv (65 header dialects, 25,278 rows) + the -DEEP.json shapes is a multiple, not a fraction;
      the offset plumbing in quote_verify.rs is ~30-60 lines because the values are already computed at the call site. Comparable prior work in this
      repo: crates/archon-lanham (the completed Python->Rust Lanham port) is 2,461 Rust LOC total, so this is roughly 2x the largest port archon has
      actually landed. The 2026-06-24 migration spec rates its own phases P0=M, P0.5=L, P1=L, P2=M, P3=L, P4=M, P5=XL, P6=M, P7=M, with P5 (line-for-
      line parity against the live TS) as the cutover gate — consistent with the line estimate. Excluded from this number: re-ingest, embedding
      backfill, and the ~9,762 distinct edge triples' semantic normalization, all of which are data work rather than code.

  SUMMARY:
      Archon-first is the right call, but for a narrower reason than "Rust is better": the overhaul is fundamentally a STORE-and-PROVENANCE problem,
      and archon is the only one of the two repos that has a store or provenance at all. Measured: archon runs a 987 MB CozoDB with 226 documents /
      17,824 chunks / 17,744 pages, all 17,824 indexed (`archon docs status`), with `doc_chunk_blocks{char_start,char_end,page,x0,y0,x1,y1}` and
      `doc_locators{kind,value,bbox}` declared at crates/archon-docs/src/schema.rs:196-241, and a live quote-verify that returns exact/p.48/bbox
      [66,327,360,368]/Bekker 431b1 for a real quotation. The incumbent's entire index runtime is one 664 KB JSON blob read whole-file (src/god-
      agent/shared/jsonl-loaders.ts:212 `readFileSync` + `JSON.parse`, no streaming) whose 701 ontologyNodes carry zero ids, zero pages, zero quotes
      (verified by direct enumeration: 0/701 for each). You cannot attach 450,000 clause records with per-clause provenance to a record type that has
      no id.  Engineering health also favours archon, but less than the raw numbers suggest, and I want the downside stated plainly. archon's
      advantages are real: 515,197 Rust LOC across 39 crates, 8,131 `#[test]`/`#[tokio::test]` attributes in 1,215 files, five GitHub Actions
      workflows, a documented clippy remediation that took `clippy --workspace --all-targets -D warnings` from 243 findings to 0 with adversarial
      verification of 47 call sites, and `crates/archon-evidence/src/index.rs` — 977 lines, 13 named semantic tests, already a Rust reader over
      compiled-index.json implementing canonical-term expansion, tension-pole surfacing and owning-work re-rank, including
      `substring_inside_a_word_does_not_match`, a regression test for exactly the bug class the TS runtime still ships. Against that: `main` is 186
      commits behind HEAD and last moved 2026-06-13, and every workflow triggers only on push/PR to `main` (.github/workflows/ci.yml:4-7), so **not
      one of those 186 commits has ever been through CI**. Clippy is advisory in CI (`continue-on-error: true`, ci.yml:31); the one blocking gate is
      `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, and the project's own report says ~114 files are not rustfmt-clean — that gate would fail today. The four most
      recent feature commits (0057412b, 7b44c665, c3719bff, 8758f2fa) added **zero** `#[test]` blocks between them, and the last 60 commits are 55
      `fix` / 3 `feat` / 1 `test` / 1 `style`. archon's testing culture is historically strong and currently lapsed.  Where Rust genuinely helps here:
      the schema (CozoDB `:create` + serde-derived types make the 31 unenforced id prefixes and 645 free-text relation values into enums the compiler
      checks), the validator (the incumbent has no schema file and no validator anywhere — verified), and exhaustive matching over the tension-shape
      superset the migration spec already enumerates. Where it genuinely hurts: adapters over irregular text. That work is guess-inspect-adjust
      against 883 markdown files, 271 CSVs in 65 distinct header dialects, and a dozen JSON shapes, and a REPL beats a compiler at it. Cargo.lock
      lists 1,061 packages, target/ is 21 GB, and ci.yml budgets 45 min (ubuntu) / ~28.5 min (macOS) for a full-workspace nextest run. My honest
      recommendation is the split the repo's own 2026-06-24 migration spec already implies: put the store, the schema, the validator and the query
      surface in archon; let the adapters emit a normalized intermediate and keep them wherever iteration is fastest. Two things must be fixed before
      any of it starts, or archon-first makes things worse: archon carries its own tracked copy of the index (1,731 files, `index/`) that is already
      13 days and 31 nodes stale against the incumbent's, and `main` needs to catch up so CI means something again.

======================================================================================================================================================
VERDICT: STRONGLY-FAVOURS
  scope: Consumer-side impact analysis in /home/dalton/projects/claudeflow-testing of making archon-cli the producer of corpus/index/compiled-
  scope: index.json. Covers: (a) full enumeration of every consumer of the artifact in the incumbent with the exact fields each reads; (b) the byte-
  scope: level legacy view contract archon must emit for zero TypeScript changes; (c) the risk direction of a producer swap given 157 tsc errors and
  scope: zero tests on these modules; (d) a dual-producer diff path before cutover. Verified read-only against the working tree on 2026-07-29 with the
  scope: prebuilt archon 1.3.11 (7b44c665) binary, npx tsc --noEmit, and python3 inspection of the live artifact. NOT covered: sizing the claim/clause
  scope: extraction itself over 226 documents; I did not open the five markdown parsers line by line beyond the offset question.

  PORT COST:
      Legacy-view producer swap only (the question in scope): ~350-450 Rust lines in archon + ~150 Python lines for the differ + ZERO TypeScript
      lines.  Basis, itemized against real code rather than intuition: - Serialize derives + emitter: the four structs already exist as Deserialize at
      crates/archon-evidence/src/index.rs:36-104 (69 lines) — a derive change, not authoring. The write path has a working precedent at crates/archon-
      evidence/src/pack.rs:239-255 (17 lines: create_dir_all + to_string_pretty + "\n"). Estimate ~200-300 impl lines. - Logic to port from Python: 94
      lines total — build_canonical_terms (compile-corpus-index.py:1285-1304, 20 lines, the (-len, lower) sort), deduplicate_ontology_nodes
      (:1311-1357, 47 lines, the name-keyed merge with "; "-joined text and sorted alias/unit unions), and main's emit (:1360-1386, 27 lines). -
      Entry-ordering table: 19 TEXT_DIRS keys (compile-corpus-index.py:37-128) transcribed as data, ~40 lines. - Tests: ~150 lines, anchored by one
      round-trip test (read the frozen artifact, emit, assert byte-identical) that subsumes the entire contract. - Differ: ~150 Python lines, reusing
      verbatim the assertions I ran in this session.  Comparable prior work in the same crate, as the calibration anchor: crates/archon-
      evidence/src/index.rs is 674 impl + 302 test lines (mod tests at :675) and delivers the strictly larger job — parse plus greedy longest-match
      expansion plus tension surfacing plus owning-work routing, with 14 tests including two #[ignore] real-index integration tests. crates/archon-
      evidence/src/pack.rs is 259 impl + 189 test (mod tests at :260). An emitter that reverses an already-written parser sits comfortably below both.
      Confidence: high on the emitter, because the contract is fully pinned (one key order per record type, all-string scalars, no nulls, two explicit
      sort rules) and the parsing half already exists and passes tests.  Explicitly NOT in this figure and not inferable from it: the claim/clause
      extraction over 226 documents, and the archon-draft QuoteEntry provenance gap. Both are unestimated above and would dominate any total.

  SUMMARY:
      Yes — build it in archon, but gate the cutover on a byte-level differ, not on tsc.  The consumer surface is far smaller than the repo's size
      suggests. Eleven live consumers, all funnelling through two loaders, reading a total of 22 distinct fields across four arrays. Ten of the eleven
      never touch a record shape at all beyond those 22 fields, and the entire artifact is 664 KB of uniform, all-string JSON with zero nulls and one
      key order. That makes a byte-identical legacy view a genuinely achievable target rather than an aspiration — I pinned the complete contract,
      including the two sort rules, the array-order tiebreaks, and the emission flags.  Two consumers were missing from the brief: scripts/merge-
      reasoning-edges.py and scripts/reason/reason_over_knowledge.py. The first matters because it reads node["name"] with a bare subscript — a hard
      KeyError, the only non-silent failure anywhere in the consumer set.  On the risk question the honest answer cuts both ways, and the popular
      reading is wrong. The 157 tsc errors are irrelevant here: I checked file by file and not one lands in the index path. Every index module
      typechecks clean. But that cleanliness is a mirage, because every entry point widens to `any` (`loadCompiledIndex(): any | null`, `parsed as
      CompiledIndex`, `(index as any).ontologyNodes`) — a cast is not a check, and tsc would not catch a renamed field, a missing field, or a null.
      Meanwhile there are zero tests across 436 test files, zero CI references, and seven separate silent-catch layers. A malformed archon-emitted
      file yields a successful run with a quietly smaller prompt: no exception, no log line, no non-zero exit. So: less risky at the type level,
      considerably more risky at the behavioural level. The swap is safe only with a differ.  Two bug-compatibility landmines will bite anyone who
      writes a "cleaner" emitter. I verified 31 of 72 hooks have an empty targetConcept, which makes `t.includes("")` always true — those 31 fire for
      every topic, and filling them in silently changes bridge selection on every run. And the tag ranking tests for the substring "medium" while the
      live values are INTERP-med — so med and low currently score identically. Normalizing either one is an invisible behaviour change with no test to
      catch it.  The case for archon is one-sided on substrate rather than on preference. archon holds 987 MB of CozoDB with 226/227 documents, 17,824
      chunks and 17,744 pages ingested; claudeflow-testing/.archon is 28 KB and empty. The goal's core requirement — character offsets for 450,000
      clause records — already exists as schema (doc_chunk_blocks with char_start/char_end/page/bbox), while the Python compiler contains no
      positional API that survives into a record: its seven .start() calls are all consumed immediately to slice section boundaries. And archon
      already reads this exact artifact in 977 tested Rust lines, live. I confirmed the provenance end to end: verify-quote returned exact match,
      similarity 1.000, p.48, bbox [66,327,360,368], Bekker 431b1.  One finding deserves attention on its own: archon-cli already carries its own copy
      of the index at index/compiled-index.json, dated 2026-07-16 with 670 nodes / 1663 terms — exactly the incumbent's HEAD, while the incumbent's
      worktree sits at 701 / 1722 and is uncommitted. Two producers of truth already exist and have already drifted. That is an argument for
      consolidating, but it also means no diff is meaningful until the baseline is frozen — which is the first step, and it needs a commit decision
      that is currently an open question in the handoff.  The main thing I would flag as unresolved by this work: archon-draft's QuoteEntry carries no
      chunk_id, offsets, bbox or match_kind, and Verification is a date rather than a content check. That is the last hop before the drafting protocol
      consumes quotations. Overhauling the index does not fix it, and if it is not scheduled alongside, the new index's precision dies at the pack
      boundary. Related trap: REPORT_FLOOR is 0.60, so a fabricated quote still returns found:true — any gate must key on match_kind, not on found.

