VERDICT: STRONGLY-FAVOURS
  scope: Costing the corpus/index asset (27 entry dirs, 1,857 files, 45 MB at /home/dalton/projects/claudeflow-testing/corpus/index/) for readability
  scope: from archon-cli: real layout-family census, audit of /home/dalton/projects/claudeflow-testing/plans/archon-corpus-index-migration-spec.md
  scope: against the tree, Rust adapter/LOC estimate with the JSON-vs-markdown reduction quantified per entry, and archon's path/root assumptions for
  scope: a cross-repo read. Verified live against archon 1.3.11 (7b44c665), HEAD 8758f2fa, prebuilt debug binary. Read-only throughout; no build, no
  scope: ingest, no writes.

  PORT COST:
      **~1,700–2,760 new lines of Rust (most likely ~2,200), plus ~700 test lines, to read all 27 entries into canonical concept/claim/edge/tension
      records.** Every figure has an in-repo measuring stick.  Anchors measured in archon-cli: - `crates/archon-evidence/src/index.rs` = 977 lines
      (673 code + 304 test, `#[cfg(test)]` at :674) for a reader over 4 record types from 1 JSON shape plus an inverted index and 3 query behaviors.
      This is the unit of measure; the whole job is ~3–4x it. - `crates/archon-docs/src/schema.rs` = 644 lines for ~15 Cozo relations incl. an HNSW
      template → ~43 lines/relation. - `crates/archon-knowledge/src/schema.rs` = 211 lines; `run_create` additive-or-ignore at :149–159 is reused, not
      rewritten. - `scripts/compile-corpus-index.py:195–773` = 579 Python lines doing exactly the markdown ontology scraping that must be replaced for
      the non-JSON entries.  Breakdown: - **A. `corpus_*` Cozo schema — 400–650 lines.** Spec §1 names 13 relations + 1 vector relation; 14 × 43
      lines/relation ≈ 600. - **B. CSV dialect normalizer — 150–250 lines.** Basis: 41 dialects over 219 files / 19,770 rows, 100% name-addressable; a
      HashMap synonym table (~60 tokens → 10 canonical fields) plus a `csv::Reader` loop. Prior art: `crates/archon-trading/src/ohlcv.rs:96`. NOT 41
      adapters. - **C. Ontology-JSON adapter, 12 entries — 120–200 lines.** Basis: 11 flat arrays + 1 nested; one serde struct with aliases + 1
      special case. - **D. Unit/per-chapter payload JSON adapter, 22 entries / 493 files — 400–650 lines.** Basis: 9 recurring payload keys (sections
      164 files, concepts 137, interlocutors 135, tensions 133, readings 101, constructs 89, positions 42, claims 31, key_terms 19) × ~50 lines each.
      The measured 50/53/41 record-shape variance is absorbed by aliases plus `#[serde(flatten)] extra: Map<String,Value>` for the bespoke tail — the
      top-1 shape already covers 44–61% per key, and the tail is per-entry crown-jewel keys (Burke's `aristotle_exegesis_table`, Calleja's
      `replaces_prior_construct_table`) that land in `extra` at zero marginal cost, or ~300 lines more if promoted to typed relations. - **E. Markdown
      fallback, 9 entries + Aristotle's 10 `*-ontology.md` — 400–600 lines.** Basis: the incumbent's 579 Python lines cover the same two forms;
      `pulldown-cmark 0.12` is already a workspace dep (`Cargo.toml:57`), so no regex table-walking. - **F. Importer binary + idempotency + row-count
      verification — 200–350 lines.** Basis: spec §7/P0 with `run_create` reused. - **G. Cross-repo path plumbing — 30–60 lines across 3 files.**
      Basis: `DEFAULT_INDEX_PATH` is a single const at `crates/archon-evidence/src/index.rs:25`, consumed at `src/command/evidence.rs:154` and `:218`;
      `CompiledIndex::load` is already path-generic; `ARCHON_INDEX_PATH` already exists as a test hook at `index.rs:804`.  Test lines: at the repo's
      own observed ratio (304/977 ≈ 31%), ~700.  **Per-entry JSON-vs-markdown reduction.** The incumbent spends 579 Python lines on 19 entries ≈ 30
      lines/entry for ontology extraction, and that number grows with every new layout. The 12 JSON-twin entries collectively cost ~150 lines of serde
      ≈ 12 lines/entry, and that number does **not** grow with entry count. Net: 12 of 21 ontology-markdown files (~57% of the scraping surface)
      disappear, and it is the high-payload 57% — GM 56 nodes, Boredom Secondary 68, VR Pedagogy 60, In-Game 44, RM 39, Uncomfortable 36, Heidegger &
      Rhetoric 36 = 339 of ~700 nodes move from regex to typed deserialization.  What this estimate does NOT cover, and it dwarfs the above:
      generating the remaining ~42,700 claims and ~450,000 clause records from the 224 ingested documents. No line estimate is offered for that here
      because nothing in either repo is comparable prior work — an honest unknown rather than an invented number.

  SUMMARY:
      Build it in archon. Three measured facts decide it, and none is a preference.  FIRST: the asset is already in archon-cli, and better cared for
      there. /home/dalton/projects/archon-cli/index/ holds 2,055 files (45 MB) of which **1,731 are tracked in git** (commits e910839c, 37c95f47).
      /home/dalton/projects/claudeflow-testing/corpus/index/ has 1,857 files of which only **335 are tracked** — just Boredom Secondary, VR Pedagogy
      Secondary, Boredom Experiment and compiled-index.json. About 1,522 files of the "irreplaceable asset" exist in the incumbent as untracked
      working-tree files with no ignore rule (`git check-ignore` exits 1). The trees are 98.2% identical (2,029 shared, 10 content-differing, 26
      present only in claudeflow, 0 only in archon) and drifting.  SECOND: archon already reads it, end to end, with provenance the incumbent cannot
      express. `archon evidence find "energeia actuality" --index` prints `index: 670 nodes / 1663 canonical terms / 64 tension edges`, surfaces
      tensions CT-01/CT-03/T1, expands to ἐνέργεια/energeia/actuality/activity/being-at-work, and returns candidates with `provenance: exact-1.00 (sim
      1.00) · bbox ✓` and `locator: 1418a1 (Bekker)`. `archon docs verify-quote "the soul is in a way all existing things"` returns EXACT MATCH, De
      Anima p.48, bbox [66,327,360,368], locator 431b1 (Bekker). Against a store of 226 ingested documents / 17,824 chunks / 17,744 pages. The
      incumbent artifact by contrast carries 0/701 nodes with an id, 0/701 with a page or locus, 25/701 with an empty definition.  THIRD: reading the
      source JSON collapses most of the adapter burden, and the burden is smaller than the spec fears. All 41 edge-CSV dialects across 219 files /
      19,770 rows are **name-addressable** — every header uses a column name from a ~60-token synonym set mapping to 10 canonical fields, so one
      sniffing table replaces 41 adapters. And the 12 `*-ontology.json` files the incumbent compiler ignores are verified 1:1 twins of the markdown it
      does scrape: across 6 sampled entries, 273/273 node names round-trip and node counts match in 5 of 6. Those 12 JSONs are 11 flat arrays plus 1
      nested, with field-synonym sets of ≤5 members each — one serde struct with `#[serde(alias)]` covers all of them.  The honest caveat, and it is
      the one that matters most: this is not primarily a migration. The tree holds 12,067 structured JSON records + 19,770 edge rows + 840 matrix rows
      = 32,677 machine-readable records, of which only **2,285 are claims and 0 are clauses**, against the goal's 45,000 claims and 450,000 clause
      records. The hand-authored entries are ~5% of the claim target and 0% of the clause target — they are the gold seed, not the deliverable. The
      other ~95% must be generated from the 224 ingested documents, and generation needs exactly what archon has (char offsets, page, bbox, exact-vs-
      fuzzy quote verification, a 987 MB CozoDB) and what the incumbent structurally lacks (a compiler that destroys offsets in the first statement of
      every parser, and a single-JSON artifact whose loader ceiling is ~25x against a ~591x requirement). Costing this decision off the migration
      alone understates the job by roughly twentyfold — and every unit of that understated work lands on the side of the repo that already has the
      primitives.

